1. Introduction

Google Dorking, also known as Google Hacking, is an information-gathering technique that uses advanced search operators to identify publicly indexed information with greater precision than ordinary keyword searches.

Security professionals use this technique during Open-Source Intelligence (OSINT), reconnaissance, and authorized attack-surface assessments to discover exposed documents, publicly accessible resources, technology references, and potentially sensitive information.

Google Dorking does not exploit a vulnerability in Google itself. Instead, it uses Google's indexing and search capabilities to locate information that may already be accessible on the web.

The technique is valuable because publicly indexed information can reveal details about an organization's digital footprint, including forgotten resources, outdated documents, development artifacts, and unintentionally exposed files.

